              {
                "text": "**Analyzing Pixel Impact**\n\nI'm currently focused on how a single non-zero pixel in an input grid influences the output. Considering an example, a 7x7 grid with a pixel at (3, 3) and a color of 5, I'm theorizing that it generates an X-shaped pattern centered on that pixel. I'm exploring the implications for both diagonals.\n\n\n**Revisiting Diagonal Patterns**\n\nI've been examining the diagonal patterns more closely. It seems like the pixel's influence extends along diagonals, which I initially thought formed an X. Now I'm starting to visualize this as a ray of light bouncing off the grid's walls. This is particularly noticeable in example 3 where the pixel's position seems to affect how it \"bounces\". This is evident in example 2, too, and I'm trying to reconcile this \"bounce\" concept with the initial X-shape.\n\n\n**Clarifying Ray Directions**\n\nNow I'm focusing on the directions the \"rays\" take when originating from a pixel, especially when they hit the grid boundaries. I'm carefully observing how corner hits cause the ray's trajectory to terminate. Example 3, with the pixel at (2, 4), is making the 'bounce' concept from before clearer.\n\n\n",
                "type": "reasoning_text"
              }
            ],
            "encrypted_content": null,
